--TEST--
`_sig` is reserved, excluded from its own input and single: signing a link that already carries one replaces it, a URL carrying two is refused rather than checked under either, and a token minted at another door does not open this one — `rule:core-api/signing-is-over-a-payload`, `rule:core-api/each-door-takes-a-different-thing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$key];

// The reserved parameter excludes itself from its own input, so a link can be
// signed again — after a key rotation, say — without one token ending up
// nested inside the next one's payload.
Core\Uri $once = Core\Uri::parse("https://example.com/a?id=7")->sign({keys: $ring, until: null});
Core\Uri $twice = $once->sign({keys: $ring, until: null});
echo $once->queryParameter("_sig") == $twice->queryParameter("_sig")
    ? "signing twice signs the same URL"
    : "the token was nested inside the second payload", "\n";
$twice->verifySignature($ring);
echo "and the twice-signed link verifies", "\n";

// Two of them is a refusal and not a choice: picking one is picking which of
// two answers an attacker gets to try, and every parser downstream picks a
// different one.
try {
    Core\Uri::parse($once->toString() . "&_sig=AAAA")->verifySignature($ring);
    echo "a second _sig accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $two) {
    echo $two->message, "\n";
}

// The same URL with the forged token *first* — so it is refused whichever one
// a reader would have reached for.
try {
    Core\Uri::parse("https://example.com/a?id=7&_sig=AAAA")
        ->withQueryParameter("wrapper", "x")
        ->verifySignature($ring);
    echo "a forged single token accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $forged) {
    echo $forged->message, "\n";
}

// One ring, three doors, and a domain octet inside the signed bytes: a token
// `Core\Signature` minted over a payload does not open as a URL signature,
// however well it authenticates.
string $payloadToken = Core\Signature::sign(["id" => "7"], {keys: $ring, until: null});
try {
    Core\Uri::parse("https://example.com/a?id=7")
        ->withQueryParameter("_sig", $payloadToken)
        ->verifySignature($ring);
    echo "a payload token opened a URL", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $otherDoor) {
    echo $otherDoor->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
signing twice signs the same URL
and the twice-signed link verifies
Core\Uri::verifySignature(): this URL is not one $keys signed. Every way of not being one — a parameter added, removed or edited, an altered token, a token minted for another door or under a key that has been retired, no `_sig` at all, and two of them — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
Core\Uri::verifySignature(): this URL is not one $keys signed. Every way of not being one — a parameter added, removed or edited, an altered token, a token minted for another door or under a key that has been retired, no `_sig` at all, and two of them — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
Core\Uri::verifySignature(): this URL is not one $keys signed. Every way of not being one — a parameter added, removed or edited, an altered token, a token minted for another door or under a key that has been retired, no `_sig` at all, and two of them — is this one sentence, so a forgery says nothing about which half of it failed.
